Docker Compose Networking
Objectives
Design and implement a multi-tier application stack using Docker Compose with explicit custom bridge networks, enforcing network-layer isolation between tiers.
Configure service discovery, inter-container DNS resolution, and reverse-proxy routing across network boundaries without exposing internal services directly to the host.
Diagnose and resolve network reachability failures using container-native inspection tools and Docker network primitives.
Prerequisites
Comfort with Linux networking concepts: IP subnets, port binding, and DNS resolution.
Familiarity with YAML syntax and reading structured configuration files.
Prior exposure to HTTP request/response cycles and the role of a reverse proxy.
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Environment Setup
Step 1.1 â Install Docker Engine and Docker Compose Plugin
Install Docker Engine from Docker's official APT repository. The Compose plugin (docker compose) ships as part of the docker-compose-plugin package and is the current supported form; the legacy standalone binary is not required.

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg

sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg \
  | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
Official installation guide if the URL above changes: https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/

sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list <<'EOF'
deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu jammy stable
EOF

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-compose-plugin
Troubleshoot this step:

Error: E: Malformed entry 1 in list file /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list â the file contains a literal backslash or newline inside the deb line.
Inspect with cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list; the file must be exactly one unbroken line. Delete it with sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list and recreate using the heredoc above.
https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/#install-using-the-repository
Step 1.2 â Configure Runtime Access and Verify Installation
Add your user to the docker group so that subsequent commands run without sudo. Apply the group membership in the current shell session, then confirm both the daemon and the Compose plugin are operational.

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker
docker version
